{
  "title": "The proposed Nahuatl endings overlap across Morelos and Puebla",
  "statement": "Tucker and Talbert used final tl and chi and several names borrowed between languages to propose a central or northern Mexican dialect. Both written endings occur in all four Gospel books of the Tetelcingo Morelos, Northern Puebla and Southeastern Puebla editions examined in 3,738 matching verse numbers. The Isthmus-Mecayapan edition and Highland Puebla treebank also have chi, with zero literal final tl in these samples. Five published Voynich label examples admit a partial mapping under spelling harmonization and a revised fish reading in some transliterations. The exact 2013 readings fail the same consistency model. The available observations leave the historical variety and its speaker community undetermined.",
  "program": "L3",
  "check": "Apply the same published spellings mechanically to connected lines using an explicit symbol inventory fixed in advance. Compare independently attested cognates at multiple word positions and whole paradigms with named early sources and present-day localities. Validate the comparisons by leaving whole documents or speakers out. Record word-level eligibility, alternative spellings and uncertainty. Assess extinction only after independent historical and community documentation makes a specific variety identifiable.",
  "on_path": true,
  "hard": true,
  "relation_to_existing_work": "P-L3-004 (#213) already checks the authors’ illustrative names and proposes inflectional tests. This follow-up measures the claimed final sequences against matched regional corpora and quantifies spelling dependence in a single-replacement reconstruction.",
  "sources": {
    "2013": {
      "authors": "Arthur O. Tucker and Rexford H. Talbert",
      "url": "https://umb.herbalgram.org/media/3jojkmox/issue100.pdf",
      "pages": "72–75, 78, 81",
      "note": "Printed p.74 invokes written tl and chi endings and shows a fish-related reading, while p.78 proposes Morelos or Puebla for the main text. The appendix parses chi as a putative lexical root in an illustrative mixed name."
    },
    "2018_text_hypotheses": {
      "authors": "Jules Janick and Arthur O. Tucker",
      "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-77294-3_13",
      "pages": "277–304",
      "note": "The public abstract says the main text remains unread and considers both a lost dialect and a mixed synthetic language."
    },
    "2018_fish_label": {
      "chapter": "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-77294-3_6",
      "page": 162,
      "quoted_by": "https://voynichportal.com/2018/09/05/not-nahuatl/",
      "note": "The precise later spelling is taken from Petersen’s quotation of p.162. The quote gives a different spelling and gloss than 2013. The enumeration is conditional on that quotation and does not adopt the blog’s dictionary interpretation."
    },
    "mouth_of_water": {
      "url": "https://nahuatl.wired-humanities.org/content/atlan",
      "note": "Classical relational form atlan ends in n, with the tl sequence in the water element, independently of the proposed picture label."
    },
    "classical_owl": {
      "url": "https://nahuatl.wired-humanities.org/content/chichtli",
      "note": "Molina and Karttunen attest chichtli, analyzed with the noun ending tli. The exact orthographic string chi alone covers unrelated forms in the measured editions."
    },
    "historical_dialectology": [
      {
        "author": "Una Canger",
        "year": 1988,
        "pages": "60–65",
        "url": "https://doi.org/10.1086/466074",
        "note": "Uses cognate correspondences and positions, including several Tlaxcala, Puebla and Morelos groupings. In some named dialects the correspondence for Classical lateral affricate is t."
      },
      {
        "author": "Karen Dakin",
        "year": 1974,
        "pages": "229, 232–233",
        "url": "https://nahuatl.historicas.unam.mx/index.php/ecn/article/download/78491/69439/231489",
        "note": "Her field reports show tl in several Morelos localities and context-dependent l and final variation in Xoxocotla. Morelos is itself a dialect network."
      },
      {
        "authors": "Robert Pugh and Francis Tyers",
        "year": 2024,
        "url": "https://aclanthology.org/2024.naacl-long.76/",
        "data": "https://universaldependencies.org/treebanks/azz_itml/index.html",
        "note": "Highland Puebla material combines speech, nonfiction and examples. Its authors describe a t correspondence for Classical tl."
      }
    ],
    "locality_catalogue": "https://www.inali.gob.mx/sitios/clin-inali/html/v_nahuatl.html",
    "tetelcingo_morelos_vocabulary": "https://mexico.sil.org/resources/archives/10947",
    "early_morelos_check": "https://www.international.ucla.edu/lai/publications/book/24",
    "early_morelos_timing": "https://www.history.ucsb.edu/wp-content/uploads/analyzing_the_cuernavaca_censuses.pdf",
    "early_central_check": "https://zenodo.org/records/7213020",
    "extinction_framework": {
      "url": "https://ich.unesco.org/doc/src/00120-EN.pdf",
      "page": 7,
      "note": "A grade for intergenerational transmission depends on whether speakers remain. INALI documents named varieties and location, a separate matter from an assigned manuscript language."
    },
    "earlier_linguistic_review": "https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-linguistics-011619-030613"
  },
  "parallel_corpus_method": {
    "selection": "All chapters of Matthew, Mark, Luke and John in four eBible HTML packages. Include a verse ID only when the displayed number is a single number equal to that ID in every edition and text remains. Remove chapter headings, navigation, section headings, references, superscript verse numbers and footnotes. Count all remaining Unicode letter/mark tokens after NFC and casefold, retaining internal apostrophes and lexical saltillo. Run a second normalization without combining marks and report type as well as token counts. Count tl, tli, tle, chi and chih as literal ending sequences, including complete tokens that match the sequence.",
    "comparability": "Parallel text controls much of the subject matter. Each edition has its own orthography, contributors, vocabulary and morphosyntax. The printed strings are not phonetic measurements or language genealogies. Report the Highland treebank as an unmatched genre check. The 3,755 verse-number preliminary match permitting bridged markers gave the same qualitative result. Remove bridged verses in the principal result. Historical works are independent follow-up controls, with different genres and orthographic conventions.",
    "editions": {
      "nhgNT": {
        "variety": "Tetelcingo, Morelos",
        "edition_year": 1980,
        "catalog": "https://ebible.org/find/show.php?id=nhgNT",
        "credit": "Wycliffe Bible Translators, Inc."
      },
      "ncjNT": {
        "variety": "Northern Puebla",
        "edition_year": 1979,
        "catalog": "https://ebible.org/find/show.php?id=ncjNT",
        "credit": "Wycliffe Bible Translators, Inc."
      },
      "nplNT": {
        "variety": "Southeastern Puebla",
        "edition_year": 2011,
        "catalog": "https://ebible.org/find/show.php?id=nplNT",
        "credit": "Wycliffe Bible Translators, Inc."
      },
      "nhx": {
        "variety": "Isthmus-Mecayapan",
        "edition_year": 2016,
        "catalog": "https://ebible.org/bible/details.php?id=nhx",
        "credit": "Wycliffe Bible Translators, Inc."
      }
    },
    "data_scope": "Local descriptive comparison using publicly readable editions. Only aggregate results and a few word examples are used here. The colonial Nahuatl, modern dictionaries and nonparallel Highland sample do not enter the four-edition counts."
  },
